1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,708
Last lecture gave you the mechanics: what gets billed, and why a long conversation

2
00:00:04,708 --> 00:00:06,993
costs more per turn than the one before it.

3
00:00:06,993 --> 00:00:10,447
This lecture turns that into an actual model, the one behind A09.

4
00:00:10,447 --> 00:00:14,752
Not a spreadsheet trick: the same number, at three different scales, and each one

5
00:00:14,752 --> 00:00:16,346
tells you something different.

6
00:00:16,346 --> 00:00:19,110
You don't need a finance background for any of this.

7
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:22,989
Every step is multiplication, and the hard part was never the arithmetic.

8
00:00:22,989 --> 00:00:27,294
It's knowing which number to multiply first, and being honest about which of your

9
00:00:27,294 --> 00:00:29,845
inputs are measured and which are still a guess.

10
00:00:29,845 --> 00:00:32,077
It's three multiplications, done in order.

11
00:00:32,077 --> 00:00:33,671
Tokens to a conversation cost.

12
00:00:33,671 --> 00:00:36,063
Conversation cost to a monthly cost per user.

13
00:00:36,063 --> 00:00:38,029
Per-user cost to total monthly spend.

14
00:00:38,029 --> 00:00:39,889
Each step looks trivial on its own.

15
00:00:39,889 --> 00:00:44,194
The gap between what step one feels like and what step three actually says is the

16
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:47,648
entire point of building this properly instead of guessing at it.

17
00:00:47,648 --> 00:00:51,793
Most teams stop at the first multiplication, because it's the one that happens

18
00:00:51,793 --> 00:00:54,079
naturally while you're testing the feature.

19
00:00:54,079 --> 00:00:58,543
You run a conversation, you check what it cost, you feel reassured, and you move on.

20
00:00:58,543 --> 00:01:02,794
The other two multiplications only happen if someone deliberately does them, and

21
00:01:02,794 --> 00:01:06,621
they're the ones that actually matter to anyone signing off on a budget.

22
00:01:07,171 --> 00:01:11,734
Walk down that table the way you'd walk into the actual budget conversation, because

23
00:01:11,734 --> 00:01:15,917
each row is the answer to a different question someone in that room will ask.

24
00:01:15,917 --> 00:01:20,480
The first row answers "does this feature work economically at all," and four cents a

25
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,273
conversation usually sounds fine.

26
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:26,782
The second row answers "is this a rounding error for one user," and seventeen cents

27
00:01:26,782 --> 00:01:28,629
a month usually still sounds fine.

28
00:01:28,629 --> 00:01:32,812
The third row answers the question that actually matters, what this costs the

29
00:01:32,812 --> 00:01:37,157
company, and eight thousand four hundred dollars a month is the number that gets

30
00:01:37,157 --> 00:01:39,982
someone's attention in a way the first two never do.

31
00:01:39,982 --> 00:01:41,123
Same underlying cost.

32
00:01:41,123 --> 00:01:44,111
Wildly different reaction depending which row you show.

33
00:01:44,111 --> 00:01:47,859
Each multiplier deserves a second's thought too, not just the totals.

34
00:01:47,859 --> 00:01:52,368
Four conversations a month is a real behavioural assumption about how often someone

35
00:01:52,368 --> 00:01:55,845
actually returns to this feature, not an arbitrary round number.

36
00:01:55,845 --> 00:02:00,354
Fifty thousand active users is wherever your product actually is today, or wherever

37
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:03,667
it's realistically headed in the window you're budgeting for.

38
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:08,068
Change either input and the bottom row moves by exactly that proportion, which is

39
00:02:08,068 --> 00:02:12,033
worth demonstrating live in the room if anyone pushes back on the number.

40
00:02:12,583 --> 00:02:17,062
Notice this is the same discipline Section four spent an entire section building,

41
00:02:17,062 --> 00:02:19,771
aimed at a cost model instead of a quality score.

42
00:02:19,771 --> 00:02:22,978
Your golden set already contains real conversation shapes.

43
00:02:22,978 --> 00:02:27,401
Run token counts against it instead of against the demo you'd show in a meeting.

44
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:30,553
What if you don't have a golden set yet for this feature?

45
00:02:30,553 --> 00:02:34,921
Name that as a real gap, not a reason to fall back on the demo estimate anyway.

46
00:02:34,921 --> 00:02:39,565
A rough token count from ten genuinely varied real-style conversations, deliberately

47
00:02:39,565 --> 00:02:43,988
including the longest and messiest ones you can construct, still beats five tidy

48
00:02:43,988 --> 00:02:46,200
ones assembled to look good in a review.

49
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:50,734
This matters because the gap between these two columns compounds through all three

50
00:02:50,734 --> 00:02:52,448
rows of the table you just saw.

51
00:02:52,448 --> 00:02:57,092
A demo-estimated token count that's thirty percent low doesn't stay a thirty percent

52
00:02:57,092 --> 00:02:57,424
error.

53
00:02:57,424 --> 00:03:01,737
It's thirty percent low at the total-spend row too, and that's the row someone

54
00:03:01,737 --> 00:03:03,119
actually budgets against.

55
00:03:03,119 --> 00:03:07,487
There's a second reason the golden set specifically is the right source, beyond

56
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:07,929
honesty.

57
00:03:07,929 --> 00:03:12,574
It's already sorted by difficulty and shape, because Section four made you choose it

58
00:03:12,574 --> 00:03:15,836
deliberately rather than grab whatever was easiest to find.

59
00:03:15,836 --> 00:03:20,204
A demo reflects whichever conversation the team happened to rehearse before the

60
00:03:20,204 --> 00:03:20,646
meeting.

61
00:03:21,196 --> 00:03:25,900
Say the honest caveat, because it's easy to present this model as more certain than

62
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:28,904
it actually is once the token maths looks this clean.

63
00:03:28,904 --> 00:03:33,664
Four conversations a month and fifty thousand active users are the two numbers doing

64
00:03:33,664 --> 00:03:37,745
the most work in that table, and before launch, neither one is measured.

65
00:03:37,745 --> 00:03:42,052
They're informed guesses, and they deserve the same treatment as the guessed

66
00:03:42,052 --> 00:03:46,472
retrieval targets back in Section five: write them down, label them clearly as

67
00:03:46,472 --> 00:03:49,986
estimates, and revisit them the moment real usage data exists.

68
00:03:49,986 --> 00:03:53,330
A model built on honestly-labelled guesses is useful today.

69
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:57,920
A model that quietly presents guesses as facts sets up the wrong conversation the

70
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:00,187
day real numbers arrive and don't match.

71
00:04:00,737 --> 00:04:05,185
That's the general move: find the closest real number you already track, rather than

72
00:04:05,185 --> 00:04:06,614
inventing one from nothing.

73
00:04:06,614 --> 00:04:11,061
A feature with no real precedent at all is the honest exception, and there the right

74
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:14,873
move is a clearly labelled range, a low and a high case, rather than one

75
00:04:14,873 --> 00:04:18,844
confident-looking number that happens to be a guess wearing better clothes.

76
00:04:18,844 --> 00:04:21,650
The friction gap in that example is the whole lesson.

77
00:04:21,650 --> 00:04:24,827
A phone call has a hold queue and a script to fight through.

78
00:04:24,827 --> 00:04:29,221
A chat window is one click from whatever the user was already doing, so it captures

79
00:04:29,221 --> 00:04:32,027
complaints a phone line never would have heard about.

80
00:04:32,027 --> 00:04:35,574
Borrow across that kind of gap and you understate real usage badly.

81
00:04:35,574 --> 00:04:39,863
Note which analogue you used alongside the number itself, so anyone reviewing the

82
00:04:39,863 --> 00:04:42,139
model can judge whether it's the right one.

83
00:04:42,689 --> 00:04:47,606
That's the model worth keeping, and the definition's last clause is doing real work:

84
00:04:47,606 --> 00:04:51,586
every number labelled as measured or estimated, not presented as one

85
00:04:51,586 --> 00:04:52,874
undifferentiated fact.

86
00:04:52,874 --> 00:04:55,567
Build it for something real, today if you can.

87
00:04:55,567 --> 00:05:00,366
Most people find the same thing: the per-conversation number feels irrelevant, the

88
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:05,107
per-user number still feels irrelevant, and the total is the one that changes how

89
00:05:05,107 --> 00:05:07,039
the room talks about the feature.

90
00:05:07,039 --> 00:05:09,322
Keep the model after you build it, too.

91
00:05:09,322 --> 00:05:13,770
This isn't a one-time exercise you file away once the launch review is over.

92
00:05:13,770 --> 00:05:18,453
The moment real usage data exists, the estimated rows get replaced with measured

93
00:05:18,453 --> 00:05:23,253
ones, and the model becomes something you check against reality on a schedule, the

94
00:05:23,253 --> 00:05:27,877
same instinct Section five's corpus-drift lecture argued for retrieval quality.

95
00:05:27,877 --> 00:05:32,793
A cost model built once and never revisited is exactly how a feature quietly becomes

96
00:05:32,793 --> 00:05:36,949
unprofitable without anyone noticing until the finance review asks why.

97
00:05:37,499 --> 00:05:39,108
One thing before you move on.

98
00:05:39,108 --> 00:05:43,769
Download the template and fill in real numbers where you have them, honest estimates

99
00:05:43,769 --> 00:05:44,657
where you don't.

100
00:05:44,657 --> 00:05:48,374
The structure matters more than having every input finalised today.

101
00:05:48,374 --> 00:05:53,035
Label the two usage assumptions specifically: if you're presenting this model before

102
00:05:53,035 --> 00:05:54,311
launch, say so upfront.

103
00:05:54,311 --> 00:05:58,639
It's a far stronger position than someone discovering later that a number they

104
00:05:58,639 --> 00:06:01,524
assumed was measured was actually a guess all along.

105
00:06:01,524 --> 00:06:06,019
Next lecture looks at the two techniques that actually bring that total-spend row

106
00:06:06,019 --> 00:06:10,568
down: caching and batching, and which one earns its engineering cost for a feature

107
00:06:10,568 --> 00:06:11,179
like yours.
